Models 

.model 
    .Python class that represents a DB table 
    .helps manage data easily using Python instead of writing MySQL
    .ORM easens connecting Python code with DB
    .helps create, update, and delete data easily 
    .supports data checks and table relationships using fields like Foreign Key 



Creating a Model
.need project and app before defining any models
    .models are then created after creating the app

        from django.db import models

        class GeeksModel(models.Model):
            title = models.CharField(max_length=200)
            description = models.TextField()
            image = models.ImageField(upload_to='images/')

            def __str__(self):
            return self.title

>>above defines a django model called GeeksModel with three fields:
    .title - character field with 200-character limit 
    .description - text field for longer input 
    .image - imageField used to store the path or reference to an uploaded image file 
    .__str__method - defined to return the title of the instance of the model when the model is printed 


    .the model is converted into a DB table through the migration system, which tracks changes and applies them to 
    the DB. 
    .django maps the fields defined in the models into table fields of the DB 



Applying migrations
    python manage.py makemigrations 
    python manage.py migrate
        

    .both commands must be executed whenever a model is CRUD in the models.py file 

    .the migrations system generates and applies the necessary SQL commands to update the DB according to the changes in the models 
        .makemigrations - creates migration files (Python code) that describe changes to the models 
        .migrate - applies these migrations by executing the corresponding SQL commands on the DB



Registering models in django admin 
.open admin.py in app directory
.register the model 
        from django.contrib import admin 
  
        # Register your models here. 
        from .models import GeeksModel 
        
        admin.site.register(GeeksModel)

        .code doesn't produce any output; only registers the GeeksModel with the admin site so that it can be managed via the django admin interface 
        .the admin panel can now CRUD model instances 

    
Model Field Types 
.fields define the structure of DB tables 
    .each field specifies:
        .type of data it stores 
        .constraints and validations on the data 
            CharField: Stores short text (like names or titles)
            TextField: Stores large amounts of text (like descriptions)
            IntegerField: Stores integer values
            BooleanField: Stores True/False values
            DateTimeField: Stores date and time
            ImageField/FileField: Stores images or files



Relationship fields 
.allow easy querying of related objects using django ORM and automatically handle DB constraints 
.used when models are related to each other 
    ForeignKey: Many-to-one relationship.
    OneToOneField: One-to-one relationship.
    ManyToManyField: Many-to-many relationship.



Field options 
.every field in models can have options(attributes) to control its behavior 
.make django models highly flexible and ensure data integrity in the DB and forms 
.they allow:
    Set constraints (null, blank, unique)
    Provide default values (default)
    Customize display names (verbose_name)
    Add help text for forms (help_text)
    Apply validators to enforce custom rules



CRUD operations on models  
.ORM allows interaction with the DB using Python code instead of raw SQL
    .create - add new records to the DB and save them 
    .retrieve - query and fetch existing records easily
    .update - modify existing records and save the changes 
    .delete - remove records safely from the DB 
    